.TH WMAKE-ZONES 1 "2026-11-05" "wmake-zones 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wmake-zones \- window zones and automatic tiling for Window Maker and wlmaker
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wmake-zones
.RB [ \-\-dockapp | \-\-session | \-\-reset ]
.br
.B wmake-zones
.I command
.RI [ argument ]
.br
.B wmake-zones-prefs
.RB [ \-\-editor | \-\-about | \-\-\fIsection\fR ]
.br
.B wmake-zones-wl
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wmake-zones
adds snap zones and automatic tiling to the Big WindowMaker desktop.
Zones are on by default and tiling is off: windows float as in Window
Maker and move only when dropped on a zone.
.PP
.B Zones:
while a window is dragged, a panel with the thumbnails of the layouts
appears first; resting the pointer on one shows its zones on the monitor,
and dropping on a zone of a thumbnail snaps there at once.
Releasing the button over a zone snaps the window into it.
.B DragFlow
changes this: the zones of the current layout right away, the panel only
with the modifier key, or at the top edge.
Near the border between two zones the target becomes both of them.
.B Esc
during the drag cancels it and puts the window back.
A snapped window dragged out again gets its previous size back.
.PP
.B Auto Tiling:
the windows of a monitor and workspace are arranged with one of the
algorithms
.BR master ,
.BR columns ,
.BR rows ,
.BR grid ,
.BR bsp ,
.BR monocle ,
.B spiral
or
.BR dwindle .
Dropping a tiled window over another swaps them; turning tiling off puts
every window back where it was.
.PP
On X11 the work is done by the
.B wmake-zones
service, which talks to Window Maker through EWMH and XInput 2.
On Wayland it is done inside the wlmaker compositor, which links the same
geometry engine and reads the same configuration file;
.B wmake-zones-wl
is the dock app for wlmaker.
The same commands work in both sessions.
.PP
The service uses no CPU while idle: it wakes up only for X events,
commands and the timers of an ongoing drag.
With zones and tiling off it does not run at all.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B (none)
Start the service (X11).
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dockapp
Start the service with its 64\(mu64 tile for the Window Maker Dock or Clip.
Left click switches between Zones and Tiling, the middle button turns
everything off and back on, the wheel cycles the layouts and the right
button opens the menu (all configurable in the
.B [Dockapp]
section).
Dragging the tile moves it like any Window Maker icon (into the Dock, the
Clip or a drawer, or out of them with the modifier); the right button on
its 3-pixel border, or the menu item
.IR "Window Maker options..." ,
opens Window Maker's own menu for that place (Dock position, monitor,
drawers, launch, bring here, hide, settings, kill; or the Clip's menu).
In a Wayland session this runs
.BR wmake-zones-wl .
.TP
.B \-\-session
Used by the autostart entry at login: the service stays only when zones,
tiling or
.B KeepRunning
is on.
.TP
.B \-\-reset
Turn zones and tiling off and give Window Maker back its own
.B WindowSnapping
setting.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the options and commands.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH COMMANDS
Commands are sent to the running service of the session (X11 or wlmaker)
through a socket in
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/wmake-zones/ .
Turning zones or tiling on while the X11 service is not running saves the
setting and starts the service.
.TP
.B status
State, monitors with their work areas and effective layouts, windows and
shortcut conflicts.
.TP
.BR mode " " zones | tiling | off | toggle | enable
Organization mode.
.B toggle
switches between Zones and Tiling (off turns the last mode on),
.B enable
turns everything off or the last mode back on.
Without an argument, show the mode.
.TP
.BR zones " " on | off | toggle
Snap zones while dragging.
.TP
.BR autotile " " on | off | toggle
Automatic tiling.
.TP
.BR layout " " \fIID\fR | next | prev
Zone layout of the monitor under the pointer (its own layout when it has
one, otherwise the general one).
.TP
.B layouts
Available layouts (built in and from the configuration file).
.TP
.BI algorithm " name"
Tiling algorithm.
.TP
.BI snap " n"
Put the active window in zone
.I n
(1 is the first).
.TP
.BR move | swap | focus " " left | right | up | down
Move the active window to the neighbouring zone (crossing to the next
monitor when there is none), swap it with the window there, or focus it.
.TP
.BR restore ", " restore\-all
Give the active window (or every snapped window) its size and place from
before snapping.
.TP
.BR float ", " promote ", " grow ", " shrink ", " arrange
Take the active window out of tiling or back in; make it the master;
change the master ratio; snap every window to its nearest zone.
.TP
.BR undo ", " redo
Undo or redo the last change: snaps, layout, mode, tiling, swaps, the
master ratio.
.TP
.B picker
Choose a zone with the keyboard: digits or arrows, Enter, Tab for the
next layout, Esc.
.TP
.BI preview " tree"
Show a layout for a moment, for example
.BR "H(2,1)" .
.TP
.BR windows " [" \-l | \-d ]
Class, instance and title of the open windows (on Wayland the class is
the app ID); with
.BR \-l ,
monitor, zone, tiling, frame geometry, class and title; with
.BR \-d ,
class, tiling and why the window is or is not tiled (for example "não
cabe: mínimo 760x200, célula 402x343": a minimum size larger than its
cell, as GTK4 and libadwaita applications often ask for).
.TP
.B overlay
State of the zones overlay and the layout panel (diagnostics).
.TP
.BR editor ", " prefs " [" \fIpage\fR ]
Open the zone editor or the preferences, optionally at a page:
.BR general ,
.BR zones ,
.BR tiling ,
.BR monitors ,
.BR appearance ,
.BR keys ,
.BR rules ,
.BR advanced .
.TP
.BR reload ", " log ", " ping ", " quit
Read the configuration again, show the last log lines, check that the
service answers, stop it.
.SH LAYOUTS
A layout is a tree of horizontal
.RB ( H )
and vertical
.RB ( V )
splits; each item is a weight with an optional quoted name, or
.IB weight : node
for a nested split:
.PP
.RS
.nf
H(1,1)                     two halves
H(1,2,1)                   centre column twice as wide
H(2:Z,1:V(1,1))            large left, two stacked on the right
H(2"Code",1"Docs")         named zones
.fi
.RE
.PP
Zones of the same weight have the same size; the gaps between them are
taken from the space before it is divided.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/wmake-zones/wmake-zones.conf
Configuration (INI, compatible with GKeyFile).
Changes made by the service or the preferences keep comments and unknown
keys and are written atomically.
Settings can be given per monitor
.RB ( "[Monitor DP-1]" ),
per workspace
.RB ( "[Workspace 2]" )
or both, and per application with
.B [Rule ...]
sections.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/wmake-zones/wmake-zones.conf.example
Every key with comments.
.TP
.I /etc/xdg/autostart/wmake-zones.desktop
Starts the X11 service at login when it is needed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WZ_DEBUG
When set, the service logs the time taken by the overlay, snapping and
tiling.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
wmake-zones zones on
wmake-zones layout thirds
wmake-zones autotile on && wmake-zones algorithm bsp
wmake-zones snap 2
wmake-zones windows \-l
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wmaker (1),
.BR wlmaker (1)
.PP
https://github.com/big-windowmaker/wmake-zones
